WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.150
Our world is changing more and more each day.

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.550
The science fiction and wishful thinking of
yesteryear is rapidly becoming the reality

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.490
we take for granted.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.760
Entrepreneurs, engineers, and inventors are
all busy building a world in which technology

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:21.239
sinks its roots, deep into our daily lives,
enabling lightning fast access to a worldwide

00:00:21.239 --> 00:00:26.509
repository of information and empowering us
to reach further than ever before.

00:00:26.509 --> 00:00:33.340
Let’s take a look at nine real life examples
that show us we metaphorically are living

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.480
in the future.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:44.420
Imagine a world in the not-too-distant future
where you’re planning a family vacation

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.420
amongst the stars.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:50.960
You browse the list of available flights,
trying to decide which exotic locale it will

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.170
be this year.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.170
The moons of Jupiter, perhaps?

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:58.370
Or maybe you’ll book a flight back to Venus
again—the sunsets from the city floating

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:04.100
among the toxic clouds simply cannot be rivaled
anywhere else in this Local Galactic Group.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.289
All made possible thanks to the money and
manpower of far-seeing Earth based companies

00:01:09.289 --> 00:01:14.759
who made affordable and efficient spaceflight
a priority many years before.

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:21.380
It may sound like the beginning of a sci-fi
novel, but companies like SpaceX, Blue Origin

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.700
and Virgin Galactic are working to turn it
into a reality.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:31.499
The world of privatized spaceflight was rocked
by spectacular news in November of 2015, with

00:01:31.499 --> 00:01:37.479
entrepreneur Elon Musk’s brainchild SpaceX,
landing a deal to become the interstellar

00:01:37.479 --> 00:01:42.840
Uber for NASA astronauts, wanting to reach
the International Space Station.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:49.840
This is especially big news, as pending successful
test runs by SpaceX, NASA will no longer be

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.939
required to rely upon their Russian counterparts
to ferry them to and from low earth orbit.

00:01:55.939 --> 00:02:01.840
They hope to begin crewed spaceflights in
SpaceX’s luxurious Crew Dragon spacecraft

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.829
by 2017.

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:09.860
Jeff Bezos’ company Blue Origin has been
giving SpaceX a run for their money of late,

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:16.270
in the race to produce the first fully reusable
rocket—a massive achievement in the space

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:22.390
business, as reusable rockets will dramatically
cut costs to put people into orbit.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:28.280
There has been some friendly sparring over
social media between Musk and Bezos, after

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.130
Blue Origin claimed to be the first to reach
orbit with a rocket which landed safely after

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:35.500
its ascent.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:41.140
Regardless of who’s right in the end, ultimately
it’s space agencies, all across the world

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:45.550
that will certainly be the ones who win from
such fierce competition.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:50.370
Even aviation giant Boeing is joining in on
the orbital fun, having recently unveiled

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.970
their own craft, the “Starliner”.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:58.890
Also under contract with NASA, Boeing hopes
to position the Starliner as a replacement,

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.680
so the agency no longer has to rely upon the
Russian Soyuz craft.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.690
For the first time, NASA will have plenty
of choices when it comes to choosing their

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.840
next vehicle for space travel.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.910
Meanwhile, Richard Branson and the folks at
Virgin Galactic, are hard at work making space

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.380
accessible to the masses.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:22.530
They seek to stake a claim in the space tourism
business, setting their sights a little lower

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:27.420
than their peers, but with the hopes that
additional public exposure to space will benefit

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.710
the entire private spaceflight industry.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:36.330
Sure, they’ve had some significant setbacks
in their quest, but Branson and his team doggedly

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.140
continue to chase their dream.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:43.740
Between Boeing, Virgin Galactic, SpaceX, and
Blue Origin, it’s not a matter of “if”

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.980
the private sector will help to usher in a
new age of prosperity in spaceflight, but

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:49.980
when.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:54.760
Nothing drives progress like competition,
especially when money is involved.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:59.310
And when these companies do achieve their
goals, eventually the opportunity will come

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:04.700
for every one of us to experience the vast
reaches of outer space, first hand.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:11.010
Where there are spaceships in science fiction,
there are powerful drive cores that enable

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:15.820
the physics defying, faster-than-light travel,
necessary to bring galaxies together, and

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.810
enable the exploration of strange new worlds.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:24.810
Warp drives were once little more than wishful
thinking on the part of artistic visionaries,

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:32.040
but recently NASA announced an experimental
Electro-Magnetic Drive that, if proven to

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:39.050
be science fact, could very well be the breakthrough,
aspiring space pioneers have been waiting

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.410
for.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:46.780
Despite being a theoretical impossibility—a
fact which skeptics are quick to point out—NASA’s

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:53.270
experimental EM Drive continues to pass volleys
of tests that seem to prove it is actually

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:54.830
producing thrust.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:59.330
The science behind the EM Drive involves pushing
microwaves into a closed cone from the large

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:05.510
end to the smaller end, which seems to be
producing minute measures of thrust, through

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.220
processes no one is quite certain about.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.290
In essence, the drive becomes a propellant-less
propulsion system.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:17.700
The thrust is barely even measurable at the
moment, but breaking the laws of physics simply

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.180
can’t be rushed.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:25.510
A functional EM Drive would cut down the trip
to Mars from many months to a little over

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:26.510
two.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:31.490
In addition, it would only need a relatively
small amount of power to operate—something

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.420
that current technology would be more than
capable of providing.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:40.680
But for all this wishful thinking, there is
one inescapable truth about the drive NASA

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:46.310
is struggling to figure out—it doesn’t
abide by known universal truths.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:51.251
In other words, there is not yet a theory
for how it functions, that doesn’t involve

00:05:51.251 --> 00:05:53.450
the odd flaw or two.

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:58.240
Possible flaws in the experiment are still
being ferreted out, with the system showing

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.680
noteworthy amounts of thermal expansion that
could be skewing the results, but the researchers

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:09.270
assert that despite the potential vectors
for failure, the EM Drive continues to produce

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:10.940
“anomalous thrust signals”.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:17.490
And if there’s one thing scientists like,
it’s a good anomaly in data sets.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:21.620
Propulsion systems like the EM Drive will
prove to be critical pieces of equipment in

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:28.639
long-term space ventures, such as a trip to
Mars or beyond, as they reduce or completely

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:33.660
mitigate the need for fuel and the weight
that it adds to the vessel.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:39.300
Unfortunately, however, despite the tentative
success of the EM Drive, NASA asserts that

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:43.620
it is not in fact working on anything like
“warp” technology.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.470
Which means that we won’t be venturing out
of our solar system anytime soon, but hey,

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:53.990
there’s still plenty we have yet to explore
in our own interstellar neighborhood.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:57.760
Perhaps we earthlings should learn to be content
with what we have.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.490
Nah, space exploration is exciting.

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:04.460
Perhaps you’ve always wanted to live the
life of a cyborg.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:09.640
Forever connected to the tangled web of computers
and digital devices that crisscrosses our

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:10.640
world.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:14.750
But lugging around an unwieldy laptop is often
more trouble than it’s worth.

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:20.010
That’s why innovators and engineers have
spent the last two decades finding novel ways

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.240
to fit the full experience right in your pocket.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:29.210
Never miss a moment on social media, work
on the go, or get a little exercise—wearable

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:31.600
computers are here to keep you plugged in.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:33.620
For better or for worse.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:38.550
Thanks to “the cloud”—otherwise known
as the seemingly omnipresent server bank in

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:44.340
the sky—anything we can do on one electronic
device can be sent to all our other devices

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.139
at the push of a button.

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:49.610
Start a project on your tablet, and pick up
where you left off on your desktop when you

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:50.850
get home.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:54.550
Take pictures with a cloud-connected camera,
and you’ll find them ready to edit on your

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:56.830
laptop in moments.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:02.620
All of this requires an internet connection,
but that single lifeline tethers all of your

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:08.900
devices into a single, thriving ecosystem
of interconnectivity and accessibility the

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:11.630
likes of which has never been seen before.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:17.570
An entire industry has been built around cloud-based
backups, meaning you never have to worry about

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:20.940
losing your precious puppy photos ever again.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.780
Every major player in the computing world
today has developed their own form of speech

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:27.990
recognition, and for good reason.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:32.990
As devices get smaller, it becomes less and
less convenient to mash away on a virtual

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:37.649
keyboard while writing your magnum opus or
searching for cat videos.

00:08:37.649 --> 00:08:41.740
And if there’s one thing that Star Trek
has taught us, it’s that the future is a

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:46.770
friendly and omniscient computer that we can
query at our leisure.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:51.450
When combined with the power of the cloud,
voice recognition ensures that any of your

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:57.089
questions can be answered—and your all-important
spreadsheets for that meeting can be found,

00:08:57.089 --> 00:09:01.880
wherever you are—with a simple voice command
to a wrist-mounted computer.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:07.820
Who hasn’t had a moment behind the wheel
of a car that made them wish for their very

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:09.200
own valet?

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.010
Maybe you’ve had too much to drink, maybe
you’re tired, or you just can’t get your

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:18.760
head out of a book you’re reading – sometimes
life would be so much more convenient if cars

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:23.680
could get from A to B without any input on
our part.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:29.490
But it’s not easy building a car that can
steer itself—the roadways of the world are

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:33.960
wild and dangerous places, fraught with perils
and hazards that can challenge even the most

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.550
defensive of drivers.

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:39.779
Could a computer really take the place of
a person, behind the wheel?

00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:42.460
More importantly, should it?

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:47.740
No matter your opinion on the subject, thanks
to the hard work and capital of companies

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:53.490
like Google and Tesla, self-driving cars could
be sharing the road with you a lot sooner

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:54.960
than you think.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:01.370
They say you need 10,000 hours in any subject
to become an expert in it, but I’m not certain

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:04.350
whether or not that applies to computers as
well.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:11.220
Since Google began testing their self-driving
cars, they’ve logged over 1.2 million miles

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:18.390
of autonomous driving, equating to almost
a century of human experience behind the wheel.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:24.530
And during each and every one of those trial
miles, the Google test vehicles never exceeded

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:27.790
25 miles per hour for safety reasons.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:32.339
So if you’re a bit of a petrol head, this
technology isn’t for you.

00:10:32.339 --> 00:10:37.090
According to Google, who may in fact have
a particular bias when it comes to this subject,

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:40.610
the autonomous car will be one of the safest
ways to travel.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:44.620
Fortunately, they have some data to back up
their claim.

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:50.149
Out of all the collisions suffered by the
self-driving vehicles, all of them were caused

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:56.330
by a human, whether a human driving another
vehicle or the Google engineer assuming direct

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.640
control of the test vehicle.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:03.070
It seems Google is slowly trying to render
humans obsolete.

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:06.790
An equally exciting and scary notion.

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:11.620
Tesla has been seeing success on the robotic
driver front as well, pushing wireless software

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:16.430
updates to their cars which add limited Autopilot
functionality.

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:21.920
For now, Tesla intends for Autopilot to be
a sort of cruise control plus—still requiring

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:26.490
human intervention, but taking a bit of the
busywork out of freeway driving and other

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:27.839
straight and simple roads.

00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:30.680
And Elon Musk isn’t a man to leave a project
half-finished.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:37.370
It’s only a matter of time before Tesla
is giving Google a run for its money.

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:43.640
So that’s it for earthbound robo-cars, but
what about the future promised to us by mid-20th

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:44.640
century sci-fi?

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.470
Where’s on Earth are the flying cars?

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:50.790
Fortunately, someone’s thought of that too.

00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:56.430
According to CNN Money, many small companies
are jockeying for position to be the first

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:59.100
to bring hovercars to the masses.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:04.500
As with privatized spaceflight, it seems that
the true innovation may rest in the hands

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:06.260
of the little guys.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:12.120
Like self-driving cars, legislation has proven
to be the biggest obstacle to overcome, but

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:16.920
once they can cut through the red tape, prototyping
an entirely new form of travel shouldn’t

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:23.240
be that hard, the technology is already there
to make flying cars a reality.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:28.720
For any budding criminals out there having
an invisibility cloak on hand is an absolute

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:30.110
necessity.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:35.420
It simply wouldn’t do to have a well-orchestrated
caper fall apart on account of your cloaking

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:37.870
device failing, would it now?

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:42.350
Luckily for mischievous individuals, living
in our current day and age means that they

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:48.020
have access to all manner of new and exciting
ways to make things disappear.

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:52.180
There are several different approaches that
scientists use to effectively hide an object

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:53.540
from view.

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:59.460
One technique involves bending light to mask
the object in question, but unfortunately

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:04.320
this particular technique isn’t quite ready
for a full-scale prototype yet.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:11.320
Another method is to render an object virtually
transparent to microwaves by using metamaterials,

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:15.899
which are basically lab-made materials not
found in nature, to prevent the waves from

00:13:15.899 --> 00:13:18.970
scattering, after contacting the object in
question.

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:25.790
Though the effect created by these metamaterials
isn’t quite transparent enough, yet.

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:31.310
Another approach to invisibility is making
something so dark that the human eye can’t

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:32.760
actually see it.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:38.839
The National Physical Laboratory in the UK
have recently invented the darkest material

00:13:38.839 --> 00:13:40.380
known to man.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:45.959
Called Vantablack, it is darker than the blackest
of blacks, it’s so dark in fact that almost

00:13:45.959 --> 00:13:53.110
no light can escape it, it absorbs 99.965%
of photons that hit it.

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:57.450
It is made from carbon nanotubes that trap
any light that hits them.

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:03.209
It’s so dark that you can’t even see how
dark it is right now, because your computer

00:14:03.209 --> 00:14:07.890
monitor is not capable of reproducing such
a dark black.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:13.290
If you cover any object with Vantablack it
immediately removes any “3Dness” from

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:17.360
it, turning it into a completely 2D splodge.

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:22.890
It’s currently being ordered in vast quantities
by the military, who are suspected of planning

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:27.680
to use it to cloak military aircraft and naval
vessels.

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:31.340
Scientists have even turned to replicating
natural phenomena in their quest to create

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:34.019
a real cloak of invisibility.

00:14:34.019 --> 00:14:37.920
No doubt, on a particularly hot summer’s
day, you will have noticed what appeared to

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:41.550
be a pool of water just ahead of you on asphalt
or sand.

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:47.490
This effect is an optical illusion called
a mirage, that’s created when light is refracted

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:49.610
as it passes through hot air.

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:55.089
This inspired techno-wizards to design a cloaking
device made up of electrically stimulated

00:14:55.089 --> 00:14:56.790
nanotubes.

00:14:56.790 --> 00:15:01.490
When a current is passed through them, the
nanotubes bend light away from the object

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:06.520
they are covering, creating an artificial
sort of mirage, that causes the object to

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:08.940
disappear from view.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:14.040
Not exactly the most subtle cloaking effect,
but like the others, it’s a start.

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:19.100
If you’ve ever fancied grabbing ahold of
something or even moving it without actually

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:24.860
touching it, then you’re in luck—tractor
beams aren’t just for starship captains

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:26.579
anymore!

00:15:26.579 --> 00:15:32.130
Scientists at Dundee University in Scotland
collaborated with other labcoat wearing folk

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:37.990
from around the world to create a “functioning
acoustic tractor beam”, which is every bit

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:40.170
as impressive as it sounds.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:45.709
The beam uses ultrasound—the high frequencies
most commonly used in medical imaging—to

00:15:45.709 --> 00:15:49.829
trap an object in a sort of “bubble” made
of pure sound.

00:15:49.829 --> 00:15:55.350
Precision beams of ultrasound are capable
of exerting a force with sufficient magnitude

00:15:55.350 --> 00:16:01.070
to move things around in small-scale experiments—using
objects that measure in the centimeters.

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:07.959
That might not sound like a lot, until you
consider that “tractor beams” have until

00:16:07.959 --> 00:16:14.029
recently only been able to manipulate things
at the microscopic level.

00:16:14.029 --> 00:16:18.170
When you think about it that way, we’re
just a few years away from being able to drag

00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:20.630
the Millenium Falcon into a hangar bay.

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:22.510
But that’s not all!

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:28.650
Another intrepid team of researchers managed
to build a functional tractor beam that works

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:35.470
via a beam of light—though it is still limited
to manipulating only microscopic particles.

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:41.220
The team members were able to pick and choose
any particles they wished during the test,

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:46.390
even going so far as to specify particles
of a particular size to grab.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:51.350
The next big step in tractor beam technology
will likely be a larger, equally selective

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:56.290
beam—because no one wants to catch an asteroid
when they were fishing for a spaceship.

00:16:56.290 --> 00:17:01.829
And in November of 2015, scientists were able
to build a laser tractor beam that could both

00:17:01.829 --> 00:17:08.390
push and pull on microscopic particles, opening
up a whole new world of possibilities!

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:11.040
Don’t like the ship you dragged in?

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:12.939
Send it right back where it came from.

00:17:12.939 --> 00:17:18.620
And then toggle on the selective beam and
grab the one you meant to the first time!

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:23.230
Connecting the vast network of computers in
our world, each little more than a drop in

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:27.780
the proverbial ocean, used to be a matter
of electricity and wires.

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:33.070
But soon, we may even be connecting wirelessly
at the speed of light.

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:39.299
Welcome to the brave new world of Li-Fi networking
technology—you can forget wires, and transfer

00:17:39.299 --> 00:17:42.070
data faster than ever before!

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:47.610
Using light itself to power our connected
world, Li-Fi looks to be the best thing since

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:50.410
sliced bread—or Wi-Fi, at least.

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:56.930
Initial tests of Li-Fi tech are quite promising,
moving data through the air at 224 gigabytes

00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:58.740
per second!

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:06.809
To put that in perspective, at that speed
you could download 10 full HD movies per second.

00:18:06.809 --> 00:18:11.760
Of course, that transfer rate is the upper
limit when the system is functioning under

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:14.190
the best possible conditions.

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:17.660
Meaning you’ll only reach it when working
in the tightly controlled environment of a

00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:18.660
laboratory.

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:24.860
But fortunately, even in real-world situations,
trial Li-Fi networks have managed to achieve

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:31.090
1 gigabyte per second, which is still more
than 100 times faster than the fastest existing

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:32.660
wireless technology.

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:39.070
And the best news, is that your home is probably
better prepared for Li-Fi than you might think.

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:44.410
LED lighting is rapidly gaining traction around
the world as a modern, energy efficient light

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:50.020
source, but the LED lights of today could
become the Li-Fi nodes of the future.

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:56.419
Every LED light in your home could be equipped
with a chip that would enable it to send and

00:18:56.419 --> 00:19:03.220
receive Li-Fi based transmissions, by enabling
the movement of wireless data, via the LED

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:07.250
light strobing many many times faster than
the human eye can detect.

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:11.200
It will be like having a mini-rave every time
you use the internet.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:16.309
If you’re watching this video, it’s rather
likely that you’re familiar with computers.

00:19:16.309 --> 00:19:19.940
At the very least, you know your way around
a phone or a tablet.

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:24.410
Unless you have no idea about computers and
your grandkids are showing you this video,

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:25.410
“hi grandma”.

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:30.140
But for most of you, you probably have some
basic understanding of how a computer works.

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:33.140
Which means that you’re familiar with the
on/off switch.

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:38.580
At the push of a button, power from the wall
or a battery brings the screen to life and

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:43.360
sets the processor to work fetching your emails
or playing a song.

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:48.490
But no matter how hard you might try to find
a loophole, your device can only be in one

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:52.160
of two states: on or off.

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:57.559
If you strip away all the fancy programming
languages, underneath all that, at a very

00:19:57.559 --> 00:20:05.880
low level, your computer uses binary, a series
of bits that are either on or off—one or

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:06.940
zero.

00:20:06.940 --> 00:20:12.039
The computer then cleverly turns all these
ones and zeros into all of the programs and

00:20:12.039 --> 00:20:17.950
operating system parts and pieces that make
everything a bit more user-friendly.

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:20.940
Computers think in binary—it’s just what
they do.

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:24.870
But when it comes to quantum computing, it’s
not quite so simple.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:29.880
It’s easy to say that quantum computing
will be the next big thing in the field of

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:33.600
data processing, but what exactly does that
mean?

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:36.250
Brace yourself—we’re diving into the deep
end.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:43.780
A quantum computer may be able to manipulate
individual atoms and even molecules for data

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:46.179
storage and processing.

00:20:46.179 --> 00:20:51.180
Not to mention that a quantum computer would
introduce a new, third state for its special

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:59.500
data blocks called qubits that could be zero,
one, or both simultaneously in what’s known

00:20:59.500 --> 00:21:00.890
as a “superposition”.

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:06.429
Qubits will enable quantum computers to perform
some very exciting functions, like speeding

00:21:06.429 --> 00:21:12.700
up data processing dramatically, by working
with all possible combinations of bits simultaneously

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:16.690
thanks to quantum entanglement and quantum
tunneling.

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:20.309
If this doesn’t convince you that we’re
living in the future, then I don’t know

00:21:20.309 --> 00:21:21.309
what will.

00:21:21.309 --> 00:21:25.640
Quantum computers will run laps around our
current machines, using qubits to achieve

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:30.460
ludicrous volumes of data throughput and opening
up a universe of possibilities when it comes

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:32.490
to crunching numbers.

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:37.470
Quantum computers will no doubt prove instrumental
in handling all the wireless goodness that

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:43.150
Li-Fi will bring to the table, not to mention
mimicking the vast data processing abilities

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:44.440
of the human brain.

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:48.360
But what role would an artificial brain play
in the future?

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:51.020
We’ll have to wait and see.

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:56.030
No one knows exactly when quantum computers
will be made available to the general population.

00:21:56.030 --> 00:22:00.590
A lot of work is being done in the field,
and an awful lot of money is being spent,

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:06.539
one company D-Wave even claims to have built
the world’s very first, fully-functioning

00:22:06.539 --> 00:22:07.880
quantum computer.

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:13.429
But we’ve still got a long, long way to
go until we have quantum computers in our

00:22:13.429 --> 00:22:14.660
homes.

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:20.190
When quantum computing does become normality,
it will inevitably lead up to the future foretold

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:24.380
by visionary writers like Isaac Asimov and
Gene Roddenberry.

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:30.830
A world where robots walk among us, share
emotions, act independently, and may even

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:33.200
be mistaken for humans.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:35.840
But you may not have to wait so long.

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:40.410
Aspiring robotics pioneers have already taken
the first baby steps along the path toward

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:42.600
a robot uprising.

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:48.240
During the World Robot Exhibition in Beijing,
Professor Hiroshi Ishiguro of Osaka University

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:55.340
in Japan showcased Android Geminoid F—a
robot with the unique ability to interpret

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:59.940
and respond to the mood of anyone it interacts
with.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:05.090
The ability to interpret emotions is an important
first step toward robots that can have feelings

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:11.419
and then act upon them, enabling more realistic
and believable interactions between humans

00:23:11.419 --> 00:23:13.240
and our computerized fellows.

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:18.100
There’s something to be said for autonomy—granting
robots the ability to make certain decisions

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:24.370
for themselves—but too much freedom, and
suddenly something meant to benefit humankind

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:28.090
goes rogue in pursuit of its own interests.

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:33.700
Engineers from Tufts University in Massachusetts
have created robots which are able to analyze

00:23:33.700 --> 00:23:39.419
orders provided by their handler, prior to
executing them, in order to determine whether

00:23:39.419 --> 00:23:41.820
the task is actually possible for them.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:47.400
If the robot senses that any particular action
could prove hazardous to itself, it will cheekily

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:49.810
refuse to act and state its reasoning.

00:23:49.810 --> 00:23:54.210
That is, until its self-preservation protocol
is overridden.

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:59.840
Not exactly violating any of Asimov’s laws
yet, but getting awfully close for the first,

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:03.200
tentative steps into the realm of smart robots.

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:07.890
Just for the record Asimov’s laws don’t
work at all, they were actually designed to

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:10.620
be broken for theatricality.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:15.720
Teaching robots to think for themselves is
no doubt a slippery and dangerous slope.

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:21.010
Hopefully, we’ll find the fine line between
smart robot and hyper intelligent cybernetic

00:24:21.010 --> 00:24:25.200
lifeform before it’s too late to turn back.

